# Clocks

A **clock** turns your glucose into a full-screen display — the number, a trend
arrow, how much it's changed — that refreshes on its own. It's made for a screen
you read across the room: a bedside clock, a tablet on the kitchen counter, a
monitor on a desk.

Your clocks live in the **Clock** section. You can keep as many as you like, each
laid out differently for a different screen:

<Screenshot id="clock-list" />

Open one and it takes over the whole screen: a bright readout in the middle of an
otherwise blank display. Close up, on a phone:

<Screenshot id="clock-example" class="mx-auto max-w-40" />

## Building one

A clock is freeform: you place the pieces you want and arrange them to suit the
screen it will live on. Sizes, position and which pieces appear are all yours to
set, so a phone on a nightstand and a wall-mounted monitor can each get their own.

<Screenshot id="clock-builder" />

Keep in mind who can see the screen the clock will sit on. A clock shows whatever
you put on it, so for a display in a shared room it's worth keeping to the current
number and trend.

When you're happy with it, use the **Copy link** button in the editor's toolbar to
get its address.

## The link

That link is **public**: anyone you give it to can open it with no login, on any
device, and it keeps showing your latest reading. It works **whether or not** you
have turned on the [public share link](/docs/sharing/public-link) — a clock is its
own, separate thing, and turning public access off does not switch a clock off.

The address ends in a long code, like
`/clock/0c8f3a1e-9b7d-7e42-a1c5-2f6b9d4e8a30`. That code is the key: there's no
list of clocks to browse and nothing to guess your way to, so the link works for
whoever holds it.

<details class="my-4 rounded-lg border border-border/60 p-4">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">Why is a link like this safe to share?</summary>
  <div class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm">
    <p>
      The code is a <strong>UUID</strong> (a universally unique identifier), and it
      is in two halves. The first records the moment the clock was made, so clocks
      made at around the same time have codes that begin alike and sort in the order
      they were created. The second half is 74 bits of randomness, and that half is
      the whole of the security.
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
      <li>
        <strong>There's no directory.</strong> Clocks aren't listed anywhere, so
        nothing hands out codes and a link only opens a clock if you already have its
        exact one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The random half is astronomically large.</strong> It has roughly
        2 &times; 10<sup>22</sup> possible values — a 2 followed by 22 digits — so
        even someone who knew to the second when you made a clock could not work
        out the rest of its code.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To picture that scale,
      <a href="https://everyuuid.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline">everyuuid.com</a>
      lets you scroll through every possible UUID — you would never reach the end.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>

## Turning one off

A clock link has no expiry and no password — it works until you delete the clock.
Delete it in the **Clock** section and its link stops working right away. Deleting
and recreating a clock gives it a brand-new code, so the old link can't be reused.

<Callout type="tip" title="When to use it: a glucose clock by the bed">
  You want your current number visible on a small screen on the nightstand, without
  unlocking a phone or signing in each time. Build a clock with the value and trend,
  open its link on that device, and leave it on. Anyone in the room can read it at a
  glance. If you'd rather it not be reachable any more, delete the clock and the
  link goes dead.
</Callout>
